WEBVTT

cue-VID_U6_02-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Meet Cocoa Grove Collective, a co operative that runs a cocoa processing plant. Thick arrows spill out of the factory: smoke, waste water and jobs. Some spillovers harm neighbours, others help them. These effects sit outside the firm's own accounts, yet they shape real decisions for everyone nearby.

cue-VID_U6_02-2
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:48.000
External costs are costs imposed on a third party, independent of the original transaction. The plant pays one hundred fifty thousand dollars to treat waste water. Untreated discharge would impose four hundred thousand dollars of cleanup and health costs on the town. Social cost is private cost plus external cost: five hundred fifty thousand dollars. The price of cocoa does not include that damage, so the market overproduces it.

cue-VID_U6_02-3
00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:12.000
External benefits are gains imposed on a third party, independent of the transaction. A training scheme gives Cocoa Grove one hundred twenty thousand dollars of extra productivity. It also gives the community two hundred thousand dollars of skills. Social benefit is private plus external, three hundred twenty thousand dollars. The firm gains too, because trained workers make fewer mistakes.

cue-VID_U6_02-4
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Sustainable development means meeting today's needs without damaging tomorrow's. On screen, three pillars hold up a platform: economic profit, social fairness and environmental care. If one pillar shrinks, the platform tilts. Cocoa Grove must earn enough, pay fairly and protect the river, or the whole business becomes unstable. A fair price to farmers keeps the supply chain reliable. Clean water keeps the licence. Both protect long term profit.

cue-VID_U6_02-5
00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Ethical issues create conflicts. Wages here are eight dollars an hour; a fair wage is ten. For one hundred twenty workers on forty hour weeks across fifty working weeks, that adds four hundred eighty thousand dollars a year. Certified cocoa costs two thousand one hundred dollars a tonne instead of one thousand eight hundred, adding six hundred thousand dollars on two thousand tonnes. Cheaper inputs lift short term margin, but they may hide child labour and damage the co operative's reputation.

cue-VID_U6_02-6
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:40.000
A pressure group is people acting together to force businesses or governments to adopt policies. Campaign waves roll over Cocoa Grove on screen. Twelve thousand subscribers share a boycott call. If eight percent of five million dollars of sales is lost, that is four hundred thousand dollars. Firms can engage, publish transparent reports or change suppliers. Ignoring legitimate concerns can escalate reputational damage and invite legal action.

cue-VID_U6_02-7
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Two sides, one decision. Being ethical can improve reputation, attract customers and employees, and reduce legal risk. Loyal buyers may accept a higher price. Yet ethical sourcing raises costs, and a price disadvantage can lose price sensitive orders. Paying fair wages may force job cuts instead of fairer jobs. The balance depends on whether customers value the story enough to pay. In a downturn, cost cutting may win the argument; in a boom, brand trust can win it for this co operative and its buyers.

cue-VID_U6_02-8
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Cocoa Grove chooses a middle path. Because untreated waste harms the river, therefore the firm installs treatment and raises prices slightly, so the licence is safe. It keeps certified cocoa for its premium line and cheaper beans for standard bars. That protects margin and reputation at once. Transparent reporting turns the pressure group from an opponent into a critic it can answer. Costs rise, but sales hold and the town keeps its clean water supply for years.

cue-VID_U6_02-9
00:03:48.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Apply the idea. Cocoa Grove can buy cheap beans from a supplier that pollutes, or certified beans that cost three hundred dollars more per tonne. Which choice protects profit and which protects the brand? On screen, two paths split into cost and reputation tiles. Pick one, then see the downstream effects. A boycott could erase four hundred thousand dollars of sales, while the premium line keeps loyal buyers.

